    "number": 13,
    "title": "Use git worktrees to isolate concurrent agent sessions",
    "labels": [],
    "body": "## Problem\nCurrently, the `engineering_dispatcher.py` allows multiple concurrent bug-fixer agent sessions to run in the exact same Git working directory (the root repository) when `max_concurrent_prs` is > 1. \n\nThis causes a critical race condition where agents trample each other's work:\n1. Agent A starts editing files on branch A.\n2. Agent B checks out branch B in the same directory. Git carries over the uncommitted dirty working tree.\n3. Agent B commits Agent A's changes into its own PR (cross-contamination).\n4. Agent A's branch is unexpectedly switched out from under it, causing subsequent test failures or lost changes (as observed in a downstream project).\n\n## Expected Behavior\nThe dispatcher must isolate concurrent agent sessions. It should create and use a dedicated, ephemeral `git worktree` for each issue being processed. \nThe dispatcher should launch the LLM agent inside that specific worktree directory, ensuring complete file-system isolation from other active bug-fixer agents.\n\nUntil this is implemented, it is recommended to advise users to set `max_concurrent_prs: 1` in the workspace config to serialize work.\n\n## Location\n- `scripts/engineering_dispatcher.py`\n- `skills/bug-fixer-engineering/SKILL.md`",
    "milestone": null,
    "author": {
      "id": "U_kgDOBc86wg",
